TEXT = '''<div class="card" style="border-left-color:#0047BA"><h3>Moving Day: Two Sides of the Street</h3><p class="muted">An original two-part story for KCE Pathways</p>
<p><b>Part 1: Priya</b></p>
<p>(1) I counted forty-two boxes in the truck, and every one of them had a piece of my old life taped inside. Atlanta was seven hours behind us now. Tampa air felt like breathing through a warm, wet washcloth.</p>
<p>(2) Across the street, a boy about my age sat on his front steps pretending not to watch us. He was doing a terrible job of it. Every time I looked over, he suddenly became very interested in his shoelaces.</p>
<p>(3) "Go say hi," Mom whispered.</p>
<p>(4) "I'm busy," I said, and grabbed the heaviest box I could find so nobody could argue. My arms shook, but I carried it all the way up the driveway without stopping. If I was going to start over, at least I would start over strong.</p>
<p><b>Part 2: Dominic</b></p>
<p>(5) The new girl carried a box that was almost as big as she was. Dominic winced as she wobbled up the driveway, but she never put it down. She had a fierce, set look on her face, like someone running the last lap of a race.</p>
<p>(6) He had wanted to walk over and offer to help, but his mouth had gone dry. Last year, he had been the new kid at Bay Breeze Homeschool Co-op, and for the first three weeks nobody had said a word to him. He remembered exactly how that felt.</p>
<p>(7) When the girl came back for another box, Dominic stood up, crossed the street, and lifted the other end of a long, heavy one marked BOOKS. She looked surprised. Then, for the first time all morning, she almost smiled.</p>
<p>(8) "I'm Dominic," he said. "Your books weigh a ton."</p>
<p>(9) "I'm Priya," she said. "Wait until you see the second box."</p></div>'''

